<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Havana</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as La Habana</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Havana is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, founded by the Spanish in 1515 and moved to its present site on a sheltered bay around 1519. It became the gathering port of the treasure fleets and was heavily fortified; it was the capital from 1607. The British held it in 1762–1763, and its old town and fortifications are a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/world-heritage-site">World Heritage Site</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/havana.jpg" alt="Havana" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RenaatPeeters · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DJI_0197_crp_wiki.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Havana lies on the northern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> along the Straits of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida-keys">Florida Keys</a>, where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gulf-of-mexico">Gulf of Mexico</a> joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-ocean">Atlantic Ocean</a>. The city extends mostly westward and southward from a bay, which is entered through a narrow inlet and divides into three main harbours. The Almendares River crosses the city from south to north.</p><p>The city stands mostly on low hills. A limestone ridge about 60 metres high slopes up from the east and culminates in the heights of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-cabana">La Cabaña</a> and El Morro, where the Spanish built fortifications overlooking the eastern bay.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>In the early 16th century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a> founded the town of San Cristóbal de la Habana on the southern coast of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, at the mouth of the Mayabeque River, now the town of Batabanó. The low and swampy land caused the settlement to fail. In those years the Spanish established settlements on the northern coast, among them La Chorrera, at the mouth of the Almendares River. The town that became Havana grew up beside Puerto de Carenas, the Careening Port, in 1519, and the quality of the harbour led to the change of location.</p><p>Havana overtook <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santiago">Santiago</a> in the mid-16th century because of the advantages of its harbour and became the capital of the island. It became a fundamental place for the Spanish colonial empire in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a> and a stopping point for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galleon">galleons</a> returning to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iberia">Iberian Peninsula</a>, and walls and forts were built to protect it from naval attacks. After the Cuban War of Independence a new constitution was adopted in 1901.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Havana is the capital and largest city of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> and the seat of its government. It is the country's main port. The population in 2021 was 2,142,939. Its economy first developed on its location as one of the early great trade centres of the New World, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sugar-plantation">sugar</a> and a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/atlantic-slave-trade">slave trade</a> first brought riches to the city.</p><p>Old Havana, Habana Vieja, contains the core of the original city and was declared a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1982. Its landmarks include the Fortress San Carlos de la Cabaña, El Morro Castle, built because of the threat to the harbour from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piracy">pirates</a>, and the Fortress San Salvador de la Punta, built in the 16th century. The University of Havana was established in 1728.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Havana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Britain captured <b>Havana</b> in 1762, the Crown built a regular army and an extensive colonial militia in New Spain.</blockquote><a href="/empire/new-spain">New Spain · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Under Juan Manuel Santos, talks in <b>Havana</b> produced a peace agreement with the FARC, which voters narrowly rejected in October 2016; a revised text was ratified by Congress in November, Santos received the Nobel Peace Prize, and the FARC disarmed in 2017 and became a political party.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-colombia">Republic of Colombia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>Tourism became the third source of foreign currency after sugar and tobacco: between 1915 and 1930 <b>Havana</b> hosted more tourists than anywhere else in the Caribbean, Americans escaping Prohibition.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-cuba-1902-1959">Republic of Cuba (1902-1959) · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1763 Spain traded Florida to Great Britain for <b>Havana</b> and Manila, and the entire Spanish population of St. Augustine left for Cuba.</blockquote><a href="/empire/spanish-florida">Spanish Florida · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>In Fidel Castro’s <b>Havana</b> Declaration of 1979 its purpose was to ensure “the national independence, sovereignty, territorial integrity and security of non-aligned countries” in their struggle against imperialism, colonialism, neo-colonialism, racism and foreign aggression, occupation or domination, and against great power and bloc politics.</blockquote><a href="/movement/non-aligned-movement">Non-Aligned Movement · Aims</a></li><li><blockquote>Most of the 400 copies of the first edition were sent to the New World, and although most disappeared in a shipwreck near <b>Havana</b>, about 70 reached Lima and were sent on to Cuzco.</blockquote><a href="/work/don-quixote">Don Quixote · Reception</a></li></ul></section>
